   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to authorizing\nprivate membership establishments to have certain licenses for sale of alcohol",
    "basePrintNo": "S652",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S652-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§3, 64 & 106, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S652\n\nSPONSOR: GIANARIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nauthorizing private membership establishments to have certain licenses\nfor sale of alcohol\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow for the issuance of liquor licenses to certain private member-\nship establishments\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends § 3 of the alcoholic beverage control law\nby adding a new subdivision 39 to define a \"private membership estab-\nlishment.\"\n\nSection two of the bill amends subdivision 5 of § 64 of the alcoholic\nbeverage control law to ensure that new licensees abide by the rules of\nother on-premise establishments.\n\nSection three of the bill amends subdivision 8 of § 106 of the alcoholic\nbeverage control law to allow private membership establishments to sell\nbeverages \"only to its members and to their guests.\"\n\nSection four of the bill sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation would create a new \"private membership establishment\"\nlicense within the alcohol beverage control law. This change will create\na new category for those who are interested in opening a club in New\nYork and will allow entities which have been authorized under the\ncurrent club license as not-for-profits the option to modify their\nlicenses. The bill would only apply to certain private membership estab-\nlishments with a minimum of 100 members. This new category would be\nsubject to the same fee structure as other for-profit entities under §§\n64 or 64-a of ABC law.\n\nThis legislation would support the nightlife and hospitality industry,\nwhich is an important economic driver for the state.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2024: S9464 (Gianaris) PASSED SENATE / A10381 (Stirpe) died in economic\ndevelopment\n\n2023: Similar bill A7617 (Bronson) died in economic development\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
